WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.663
This project explores controlling stage lights with two small M5Stack
touchscreens.

00:00:05.663 --> 00:00:09.490
Here, everything happens with virtual lights on screen.

00:00:10.900 --> 00:00:12.923
The Brain chooses the settings.

00:00:12.923 --> 00:00:16.970
The Controller receives them and sends commands to the lights.

00:00:16.970 --> 00:00:19.398
This browser demo simulates their connection.

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:22.659
Move brightness to sixty percent.

00:00:22.659 --> 00:00:27.865
Both numbers change together, because the Brain sends the new setting to
the Controller.

00:00:29.267 --> 00:00:32.764
Choose a lighting pattern and start a demo beat.

00:00:32.764 --> 00:00:35.874
The bars represent low to high sound frequencies.

00:00:35.874 --> 00:00:38.594
The lights react to generated audio data.

00:00:38.594 --> 00:00:40.926
The demo beat itself is silent.

00:00:42.333 --> 00:00:44.392
Disconnect, then lower the brightness.

00:00:44.392 --> 00:00:50.979
The Brain changes, but the Controller keeps its previous setting because
new commands cannot reach it.

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:53.728
Reconnect the devices.

00:00:53.728 --> 00:00:56.826
Missed changes are not sent again automatically.

00:00:56.826 --> 00:01:01.252
Move brightness to eighty percent to send a fresh setting.

00:01:01.252 --> 00:01:03.022
Both devices agree again.

00:01:04.433 --> 00:01:08.180
The Controller also has its own lights-off button, called blackout.

00:01:08.180 --> 00:01:13.425
Even without a connection, it can turn every virtual light off, then
restore them.

00:01:14.833 --> 00:01:18.654
Try the guided steps yourself, or explore freely.

00:01:18.654 --> 00:01:20.564
This shows software behavior.

00:01:20.564 --> 00:01:26.295
Real wireless connections, audio input, and physical lights need
separate hardware testing.
